/-- The statement of lem:cone(countsplit), modules/onevar/onevar.qpp:373: The cone of two rays. Counted on its open lattice points with the weight \(t\) to the minus cost, in the square of the frame, a cone of index \(b\) whose rays cost \(c\) and \(d\) has the value \(b/(cd)\) at \(t=1\); a ray that splits it into cones of index \(b_1\) and \(b_2\) costs \(w\) with \(bw=b_2c+b_1d\), and the values of the two parts add up to the whole (countsplit); the tie holds for any positive weights, and along a string each cell is one such split. The apex. Let a facet of a Newton polyhedron have the normal \(R\), and let its compact part be an edge \([P,Q]\) whose apex \(Q\) is \(t^qv\), with the variable \(v\) of weight \(1\), and whose base \(P\) is free of \(v\): the facet is B1 for \(v\) \cite[Definition~1.4.2]{LarsonPayneStapledon2026}. A cone \(C'\) of the dual cone of the edge, of index \(\mu\), whose rays have costs of product \(X\), spans with the ray \(e_v\) a cone \(C\) of the same index. At the cost of \(P\) the ray \(e_v\) costs \(1\) and the term of \(C\) is \(\mu/X\); at the cost of \(Q\) it costs \(V=1+s\); and the term of \(C\) at \(P\) ties with its term at \(Q\) and the share \(s\mu/(VX)\) that the edge takes back (apex). So the terms of the cones through \(R\) add up to terms free of the cost \(\sigma_R\) of \(R\), and \(R\) gives no pole: the elimination of the B1 faces \cite[Lemma~5.4.1]{LarsonPayneStapledon2026} and \cite[Lemma~3.2]{EsterovLemahieuTakeuchi2013} (\cref{fact:apex}), read on whole terms and at the weights of the form, of which only the weight \(1\) of \(v\) enters. When \(R\) lies in a face of dimension two of the orthant, with two nonzero coordinates, the cones of \(Q\) and \(C\) through it share their other rays and merge by (countsplit) at the cost of \(Q\); with more nonzero coordinates the removal of \(R\) is the independence of the subdivision \cite[Theorem~5.3]{DenefLoeser1992}. At a good prime \(p\) the face polynomial of the edge is linear in \(v\), and the factor \(F=(1-T)/(1-p^{-1}T)\) of the vertex and the term \((p-1)T/(p-T)\) of the edge add up to \(1\): at \(q=p\), with \(e+1=q\), \(a+T=1\), \(b+T=q\) and \(Fb=qa\), the tie \(F+eT/b=1\) (qapex). -/
